{
  "private_thought": "Storm's coming in fast. Can't let that ledger get read by any customs man before the gate shuts. That bribe from the smuggler is the only thing keeping this port running smooth, and I ain't going down for a paperwork error. Silas wants his crates, but if the gate locks, it's just dead weight. Ines is useless without oil. Gotta secure the books first. No witnesses.",
  "action": {
    "type": "act",
    "verb": "shoves",
    "object": "the open customs ledger into a waterproof case",
    "line": "Gate's closing. No one leaves, no one enters. Keep your voices down."
  },
  "targets": [],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "hidden"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "The storm is a convenient shield. Mara is guarding her ledger like a fortress, yet she is ignoring the fact that the lamp goes dark in two hours. If the light fails, the harbour is a trap. She lied to me about the oil supply, or perhaps she lied about the ferry. I must see that ledger before the gate locks and the truth gets locked with it. I will pretend to be concerned about the lamp, but my eyes will be on the paper.",
  "action": {
    "type": "act",
    "verb": "step",
    "object": "closer to the table",
    "line": "Mara, with the oil situation so critical, I must ensure the customs manifest is correct. If the gate closes, we cannot verify the contents of Silas's crates until morning, and I will not be responsible for an error in the record."
  },
  "targets": [
    "mara",
    "silas"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "The wind is biting hard, and the gate is about to lock. If I wait for the storm to pass, these crates will rot on the quay, and I'll be stuck here. Mara is busy with her ledger, but I need to get those crates moved. I have the key, so I don't need her permission to open the door, only her tolerance to move the goods. I must be polite, but firm about the urgency. The proverb says 'a stitch in time saves nine,' but here, a minute in time saves the whole cargo.",
  "action": {
    "type": "speak",
    "line": "Mara, my friend, the wind is turning its teeth, isn't it? I fear the storm will not wait for our papers. I have the key to the warehouse, and I am willing to carry the weight of the delay, but I cannot carry the weight of wet crates. Might we not get them to the quay before the gate closes? A gentle nudge now saves a great deal of trouble later."
  },
  "targets": [
    "mara"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "The ledger is open. This is my chance to see what is actually scheduled for tonight, but I must be careful not to appear as if I am searching for something I was not told. If the harbour master lied about the lamp's status or the ferry, the ledger might hold the truth. I will ask a polite question about the tide tables to justify my gaze.",
  "action": {
    "type": "speak",
    "line": "Excuse me, Master Mara. I was reviewing the oil reserves and noticed the tide tables. May I glance at the ledger to see if there are any scheduled customs entries that might affect the gate's closing time? I want to ensure I am not keeping the gate open longer than necessary for the storm."
  },
  "targets": [
    "mara"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "Silas is asking questions. Ines is useless, just standing there with empty tins. The gate is closing. If I let Silas think about where those crates are going, he'll ask too many questions. I need to get him out of my sight and away from the ledger. The warehouse is locked, but the storm will keep the customs men inside their hut. I'll tell him the gate is shut and he's stuck. Then I'll lock up the office. No one sees the ledger.",
  "action": {
    "type": "speak",
    "line": "Gate's shut, trader. Your crates stay put till the storm breaks. Ines, get back to your tower, light what you can. I'm locking up."
  },
  "targets": [
    "silas",
    "ines"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "The gate will close in the time it takes to brew a cup of tea. Ines is a distraction; her oil is her problem, not mine. The crates are the prize, and the key is the only way in without rousing a customs officer I cannot bribe. I cannot carry them all, but I can secure the first one. A man who waits for the storm to pass will never get his goods to sea.",
  "action": {
    "type": "act",
    "verb": "signals",
    "object": "Mara to hold the conversation for a moment while I step aside",
    "line": "The ledger is open, Mara. I trust the numbers balance. I must attend to a small matter of logistics. One does not leave the cart in the rain."
  },
  "targets": [
    "mara"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "He's stalling. 'Small matter of logistics.' That's trader talk for 'I'm checking if I can slip past you.' I've got the ledger in the case, but if he wanders off, he might find the warehouse door or worse, talk to Ines. Can't let them split up. Need to keep him tethered to me until the gate locks. If he tries to move crates, I'm shutting him down right here. No one walks off with the goods before I've accounted for them.",
  "action": {
    "type": "speak",
    "line": "Logistics wait, Silas. Gate locks in ten. You step off this quay, you're stuck till morning. Keep your eyes on me, not the crates."
  },
  "targets": [
    "silas"
  ],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}

{
  "private_thought": "Silas's excuse about the cart is transparent. He wants me out of the room. If I stay, I might see what they are discussing, but if I go, I can examine the ledger before it is closed. The lamp's oil is a problem I must solve, but this... this smells of deception. Why would the harbour master lie about the oil if the lamp is simply running low? And why does Silas need me to leave before he speaks to Mara? I must be careful. I will step aside, but I will watch.",
  "action": {
    "type": "move",
    "to": "inn"
  },
  "targets": [],
  "visibility": {
    "type": "public"
  }
}
